  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Wellbeing of Populations Affected Is Strengthened through Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM) Support to IDPs and Host Community in Tigray Region</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The conflict in the northern part of Ethiopia resumed and intensified in various parts of Tigray Region and some part of Afar and Amhara Regions in late August 2022. Resumption of the conflict consequently resulted in massive new displacement as well as secondary displacements. Damages to existing communal facilities have been reported due to the ongoing military operations around internally displaced persons (IDP) sites and subsequent lootings and restrictions. While it is hoped that the state peace agreement will lead to peace, security and enable IDPs to return to areas of origin in Tigray, it is anticipated that this may take time as areas of origin are badly damaged, affected by unexploded ordnance, and because IDPs have been stripped of assets and property that would enable them to restart their lives at home. 
 
Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) aims to further scale-up the provision of support and ensure wellbeing of crisis-affected populations in displacement-affected locations in Tigray Region through Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM). The proposed intervention includes all four pillars of CCCM including site planning and development, coordination and information management, community participation and capacity building.</narrative>
  </description>
